course: dictionary
lesson: 1017
title: Meet the bonobo
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bonobo is a kind of ape. It looks a bit like a chimpanzee, but it is slimmer, has longer legs and has a darker face. Bonobos live only in the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo in Africa.
explain: Context helps you learn more. «Bonobos look like slim chimpanzees» tells you the two animals are alike, but the bonobo is slimmer. The word «apes» in «Like all apes, orangutans, gibbons and bonobos have no tails» tells you bonobos belong to the ape family.
explain: Scientists call bonobos and chimpanzees humans' closest living relatives. Bonobos share about 98 to 99 percent of our DNA, the code inside every cell that makes us who we are.
fact: A bonobo is an ape, like a chimpanzee, but slimmer, with longer legs and a darker face.
fact: Bonobos live only in the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in Africa.
fact: «Bonobos look like slim chimpanzees», but they are a separate kind of ape.
fact: Bonobo groups are often led by females, and they settle arguments peacefully with play and friendliness.
fact: Bonobos are endangered, which means they are at risk of dying out because of hunting and loss of forest.
exercise: «Bonobos look like slim chimpanzees.» What is a bonobo? | *An ape that is slimmer than a chimp, with longer legs and a darker face | A kind of fish that lives in rivers | A big cat that hunts on the savanna | A bird of the rainforest
exercise: Where do bonobos live? | In the grasslands of England | In the icy mountains of Canada | *In the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo | In the deserts of Australia
exercise: «Like all apes, orangutans, gibbons and bonobos have no tails.» What do bonobos have no? | Legs | Hands | Arms | *Tails
test: Bonobos are endangered. What does endangered mean? | *At risk of dying out | Found in every country in the world | Very fast runners | Happy and playful all the time
test: Bonobo groups are often led by whom? | Babies | *Females | The oldest chimpanzee | Only males
exam: Why are bonobos and chimpanzees called humans' closest living relatives? | They have tails like us | They can speak words just like us | *They share about 98 to 99 percent of our DNA | They live in the same towns as people
